  "notes": "\"Brighter and more consonant than the NY norm\" is the whole thesis, and it is authored as major and mixolydian at a combined weight of 9 against dorian's 1 — the only entry in this batch with essentially no minor weight. The research calls the bass \"the loop's groove engine\", so it gets `followRootsProb` 0.38, the second-lowest here, an `anticipationProb` of 0.42, the highest here, and P8 first in `passingTones` for the octave-jumping motion it names. Tambourine and conga layers come from the source break, so `percs` runs [2,4] a bar. Swung 8ths at 0.61 and I–IV–V / ii–V–I over 4-bar loops with real changes. ⚠ The I–IV family at weight 2 is a REDUCTION of the researched I–IV–V, not a separate claim. ⚠ `medium`, and the inferred parameters are the drum velocities and the tempo centre: the producer interview documents the flip method and the record documents the arrangement, but neither pins a BPM or a hat density, so 97 and 0.55 are catalogue-level. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 50 to 49 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 50 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1.",
